---
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro";
import pageMeta from "@/data/page-meta.json";

const meta = pageMeta["/docs/behavior"];
---

<Layout title={meta.title} description={meta.description}>
  <div class="docs-content">
    <p class="docs-eyebrow">Reference</p>
    <h1>Behavior</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">
      loseta ships small, readable behavior modules next to the CSS. Twenty-two
      components include one, including all seven tranche-two additions. Input
      OTP, slider, scroll-area and sidebar stay CSS-only by design. The CSS
      stays optional and the behavior stays yours to edit.
    </p>

    <h2>What installs</h2>
    <p>
      <code>loseta add</code> copies the component CSS and any behavior modules
      declared by the item. Entry modules self-initialize on import; shared
      support modules (<code>core.js</code>, <code>listbox.js</code>) provide
      focus, dismissal, filtering and typeahead helpers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pass <code>--no-behavior</code> to keep the CSS-only install. Backfill the
      modules later with <code>loseta add &lt;item&gt; --behavior</code>.
    </p>

    <h2>The generated index</h2>
    <p>
      The CLI regenerates <code>behaviors/index.js</code> from the installed
      behavior files, just like <code>index.css</code>. Load it once per page:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`<script type="module" src="/behaviors/index.js"></script>`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Change the directory with <code>behaviorDir</code> in
      <code>loseta.json</code>; the generated index moves with it.
    </p>

    <h2>Scoped mounting</h2>
    <p>
      Each entry module imports <code>core.js</code> and registers delegated
      document listeners. Dynamically inserted markup works without a second
      init call. For a scoped root or a late mount, import the support module and
      call <code>init(root)</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`import { init } from "./behaviors/core.js";\nimport "./behaviors/dialog.js";\n\ninit(document.querySelector("#app"));`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Message scrollers and resizable groups also export
      <code>mount(root)</code> for a late-mounted subtree: each list owns a
      scoped observer, and resizable handles sync their reported values with the
      panel sizes.
    </p>

    <h2>Component contracts</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Calendar.</strong> The module renders the month, updates the
        title or native month/year selects, and handles single, range and
        multiple selection. Configure it with
        <code>data-selection="single|range|multiple"</code>, <code>data-min</code>,
        <code>data-max</code>, <code>data-unavailable</code>,
        <code>data-month</code> and <code>data-date</code> on each day button.
        Range uses first click for the start, second for the end, Shift-click to
        extend and a third click to start over. Custom day content, presets,
        date-time inputs and coordination between separate calendars stay
        app-owned.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Carousel.</strong> Previous/next, indicators, keyboard
        arrows/Home/End, <code>aria-current</code> and
        <code>aria-hidden</code>/<code>inert</code> on fully offscreen slides,
        with vertical and RTL support. There is no autoplay or looping; the
        controls clamp at the ends.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drawer.</strong> The optional direct
        <code>[data-slot="handle"]</code> supports pointer/touch swipe-to-close
        in all four logical directions. It uses pointer capture after movement,
        distance or velocity thresholds and native <code>dialog.close()</code>.
        The handle stays <code>aria-hidden</code> and non-focusable; close
        controls and Escape remain the keyboard path. Drawers without a handle
        are unchanged.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Resizable.</strong> One handle between two adjacent panels,
        pointer capture after movement, missed-release recovery,
        arrow/Home/End keys, a percentage split through <code>--panel-size</code>,
        <code>aria-valuemin</code>/<code>max</code> clamping, and
        <code>change</code> with <code>detail.sizes</code>. Arrow keys move 5
        percent, or 10 with Shift. Nested groups keep their own ratios. There is
        no persistence.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Questionnaire.</strong> The module owns
        <code>[data-slot="previous"]</code> and <code>[data-slot="next"]</code>,
        step visibility, <code>aria-current="step"</code>, the native progress
        value/text, disabled end states and focus into the new step. Validation,
        completion and a final submit stay app-owned.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Message scroller.</strong> Tracks whether the user is at the
        bottom, follows inserted messages, preserves the reading position when
        they are not following, toggles <code>[data-slot="jump"]</code> and
        dispatches <code>change</code> with <code>detail.following</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tooltip and hover card.</strong> Both use
        <code>hover-overlay.js</code>: it toggles <code>aria-hidden</code>,
        handles Escape and returns focus to the trigger. The CSS hover and
        focus-within rules remain the no-JS fallback. The tooltip
        <code>data-tooltip</code> shorthand stays CSS-only and visual-only.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Events</h2>
    <p>Behavior events bubble from the component root:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>change</code> — selection or position changes.
        <code>detail.value</code> / <code>detail.values</code> /
        <code>detail.range</code> for select, combobox and calendar;
        <code>detail.index</code> for carousel; <code>detail.sizes</code> for
        resizable; <code>detail.step</code> / <code>detail.total</code> for
        questionnaire; <code>detail.following</code> for message scroller;
        <code>detail.checked</code> for menu checkbox and radio items;
        <code>detail.value</code> (the tab id) for tabs.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>toggle</code> — dialogs, popovers, menus, tooltips and hover
        cards; <code>detail.open</code> is a boolean.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>dismiss</code> — toast items and drawer swipes;
        <code>detail.reason</code> is <code>"timeout"</code>,
        <code>"close"</code> or <code>"swipe"</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>State</h2>
    <p>
      Markup is the source of truth at rest. Behavior toggles
      <code>aria-expanded</code>, <code>aria-hidden</code>,
      <code>aria-selected</code>, <code>aria-checked</code>,
      <code>aria-current</code>, <code>aria-valuenow</code>,
      <code>tabindex</code>, <code>hidden</code> and focus. The only transient
      data states are <code>data-highlighted</code> on the active option and
      <code>data-copied</code> on a copy control. There is no
      <code>data-state</code> mirror. Calendar options such as
      <code>data-selection</code>, <code>data-month</code> and
      <code>data-date</code> are configuration and data, not visual states.
    </p>

    <h2>CSS-only by design</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Input OTP</strong> is one native input with decorative cells;
        typing, paste, mobile one-time-code autofill and validation stay native.
        Per-digit focus and auto-advance are intentionally outside the contract.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Slider</strong> is a native range control; a custom thumb or
        multiple thumbs would need a different control.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Scroll area</strong> is a native overflow container with native
        scrollbars and keyboard scrolling.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sidebar</strong> uses <code>aria-current="page"</code> for active
        navigation and the shipped dialog behavior for the mobile sheet.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Without JavaScript</h2>
    <p>
      Static content stays readable and the page's own navigation keeps working.
      Overlay panels, menus and selection widgets stay closed; the tooltip and
      hover-card CSS fallbacks still open on hover or focus. There are no
      <code>&lt;noscript&gt;</code> blocks.
    </p>

    <h2>Browser support</h2>
    <p>
      Behavior uses the same fidelity target as the CSS: Chrome/Edge 125+,
      Safari 26+ and Firefox 147+. It relies on native
      <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code>, <code>showModal()</code>,
      <code>aria-activedescendant</code>, <code>Intl</code> and
      <code>:has()</code> and Pointer Events; there is no transpilation or
      polyfill layer.
    </p>

    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Combobox filtering is client-side; async search edits the copied module.</li>
      <li>Multiple combobox chips stay app-rendered.</li>
      <li>Tabs use automatic activation; there is no manual mode.</li>
      <li>Toasts hide immediately; there is no exit animation.</li>
      <li>Calendar custom day content, presets, date-time logic and multi-calendar range coordination stay app-owned.</li>
      <li>Carousel autoplay and looping are not included.</li>
      <li>Drawer swipe starts only from the optional handle; panel-wide swiping, snap points, swipe-to-open and animated nested stacking are not included.</li>
      <li>Resizable groups use one handle between two panels; nested groups compose, with no persistence.</li>
      <li>Questionnaire validation, completion and final submit stay app-owned.</li>
      <li>
        Message scroller and toast are the only modules allowed a scoped
        <code>MutationObserver</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</Layout>
